# XSH pthread_create()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A thread ID is compared only while its thread is live or a zombie (XSH
# 2.9.2: its lifetime ends when it has terminated and is no longer
# joinable), and a new thread never reads the ID stored by its creator
# (APPLICATION USAGE: it need not be there when the thread starts).

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.001
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_attr_getdetachstate.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null attr, pthread_create() returns 0, the new thread calls
           start_routine exactly once with arg as its argument, and the
           thread is joinable (the default detachstate is
           PTHREAD_CREATE_JOINABLE): pthread_join() returns 0.
test       t/001-runs-start-routine.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.002
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The ID stored through thread equals (pthread_equal()) what
           pthread_self() returns in the new thread; two threads alive at
           once get different IDs, and neither equals the creator's.
test       t/002-stores-id.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.003
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A start routine that returns ends the thread as pthread_exit()
           would: pthread_join() obtains the returned pointer, and the
           destructor of a thread-specific data key the thread had set is
           called with its value.
test       t/003-return-is-exit.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.004
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A process whose main() returns 7 while another thread is
           blocked ends as exit(7) would: its status is exited with 7, an
           atexit() handler has run and a fully buffered stdout was
           flushed.
note       The process is a child made by fork() whose main() returns.
test       t/004-main-return-exits.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.005
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The new thread starts with its creator's signal mask: SIGUSR1
           and SIGALRM blocked, SIGUSR2 and SIGTERM not.
test       t/005-mask-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.006
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A signal pending for the creating thread only (SIGUSR1, sent to
           it with pthread_kill() while blocked) is not pending for the new
           thread: sigpending() there does not include it.
test       t/006-pending-empty.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.007
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       When the creator uses a thread-local locale (uselocale()), the
           new thread has none: its uselocale((locale_t)0) returns
           LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE.
test       t/007-locale-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.008
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       When the creator has an alternate signal stack, the new thread
           does not have that stack: its sigaltstack() query reports
           SS_DISABLE or a stack outside the creator's.
test       t/008-altstack-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.009
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The new thread starts with its creator's floating-point
           environment: a rounding direction other than the initial one,
           and FE_DIVBYZERO raised (where defined), are both seen there.
note       NOTINUSE when no rounding direction but the current one can be
           set.
test       t/009-fenv-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.010
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       TCT
since      2001
says       The new thread's CPU-time clock can be named with
           pthread_getcpuclockid() and read with clock_gettime(), and it
           starts at zero: read first thing in the thread
           (CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID), it is below the 300 ms of CPU time
           the creator used before creating it.
note       Assumes the implementation spends under 300 ms of the new
           thread's CPU time before the start routine runs; the text
           gives no figure, so this is a judgement of what "starts at
           zero" can be told from.
test       t/010-cpu-clock.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.011
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread created joinable stays joinable when its attributes
           object is set to PTHREAD_CREATE_DETACHED right after
           pthread_create() returns: pthread_join() returns 0 and the
           thread's value.
test       t/011-attr-later.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.012
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       With {PTHREAD_THREADS_MAX} reported by sysconf(), creating that
           many plus one threads (all kept alive) fails at least once, and
           the first failure is EAGAIN.
note       NOTINUSE when sysconf(_SC_THREAD_THREADS_MAX) reports no limit,
           or one above 4096. Accepts the failure one creation early,
           since XBD <limits.h> counts threads "created per process" and
           this page counts the "total number of threads in a process"
           (whether the initial thread counts is left open), and any
           earlier failure for lack of resources, which is EAGAIN too.
timeout    120
test       t/012-eagain-threads-max.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_create.013
anchor     functions/pthread_create.html#ERRORS/EPERM
class      conditional
when       TPS
since      2001
says       A caller without appropriate privileges whose attributes ask
           for PTHREAD_EXPLICIT_SCHED, SCHED_FIFO and the highest
           SCHED_FIFO priority either gets the thread or fails with EPERM.
note       What privileges are needed is implementation-defined: when the
           creation succeeds the test is UNRESOLVED (the caller had them),
           as it is for EAGAIN. Run as root, the call is made in a child
           that takes user and group 65534.
test       t/013-eperm.c
